/**
 * KERATINOCYTE SKIN CANCER: LIVING WITH IT, AND THE DECISIONS THAT ARE ACTUALLY MADE (UK and NHS focus).
 * Facts checked 25 September 2026.
 *
 * Basal cell carcinoma is the most common cancer there is and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is close behind,
 * and until this layer the site had almost nothing for the person who has one. Nearly all of these cancers are
 * cured. That is said first and plainly here, because it is true, and then the exceptions are given exactly,
 * because a page that reassures everybody is no use to the person whose squamous cell carcinoma is 8 mm thick.
 *
 * The layer is split across three records so that nothing is written twice:
 *
 *   `skin-cancer` (the family page, default export) carries what is true of any keratinocyte cancer and most of
 *   what people are actually frightened of: surgery on the face, what a graft or a flap means and what the scar
 *   does over a year; Mohs surgery as a day rather than as a technique; sun protection written for someone who
 *   still has to go outside; the psychological side, including the specific problem that the people around you
 *   have decided this does not count as cancer; and the immunosuppressed reader, above all the transplant
 *   recipient, for whom every sentence on the page is more serious.
 *
 *   `basal-cell-carcinoma` (`bccLivingSpike`) carries the central decision, which is a real choice between
 *   excision, curettage and cautery, cryotherapy, a cream used for weeks and photodynamic therapy, with the
 *   comparative recurrence and cosmetic figures from the randomised trials rather than the phrase "several
 *   options are available", and it says plainly that surgery has the highest cure rate. It also carries what
 *   follow-up is and is not offered after a BCC.
 *
 *   `cutaneous-scc` (`csccLivingSpike`) carries the part that is different: which squamous cell carcinomas are
 *   the dangerous ones, with the thickness and size thresholds from the two cohorts that established them, and
 *   then the lymph nodes, perineural spread, and the chance of a second primary.
 *
 * What is deliberately NOT here, because melanoma already has depth and this round is not allowed to restate it:
 * anything about moles, Breslow thickness, sentinel node biopsy for melanoma, adjuvant immunotherapy or BRAF
 * targeting (./melanoma.ts and the melanoma subtype records). Where a reader with a changing mole lands here,
 * the family page points at melanoma rather than half-answering. Advanced disease drugs (hedgehog inhibitors,
 * cemiplimab) belong to `locally-advanced-bcc` and `advanced-cutaneous-scc`; this layer stops where a lesion
 * stops being treatable by the dermatology department. Merkel cell carcinoma is not a keratinocyte cancer and
 * is not touched.
 *
 * Three rules were applied throughout. Every claim carries a source, read rather than recalled. No sentence is a
 * prognosis for one person: every recurrence, metastasis and second-primary figure is quoted with the cohort it
 * came from. And nothing here describes a scar on a face as trivial, or as a tragedy.
 *
 * Sources, all read 25 September 2026. The NHS pages on non-melanoma skin cancer, its treatment and its symptoms,
 * on scars, on sunscreen and sun safety, on 111 and on 999. NICE NG34 (sunlight exposure: risks and benefits,
 * 2016), recommendation 1.1.1 on the at-risk groups. The British Association of Dermatologists patient information
 * leaflets on basal cell carcinoma (updated July 2025), squamous cell carcinoma (updated April 2022), Mohs
 * micrographic surgery (updated June 2025) and skin cancer advice for organ transplant recipients (June 2024,
 * written with the British Society for Skin Care in Immunosuppressed Individuals), and the BAD guidelines those
 * leaflets rest on. Cancer Research UK's non-melanoma skin cancer section: types of surgery, problems after
 * surgery, follow-up appointments, skin care afterwards, coping, and referral to a specialist.
 *
 * And the trials and cohorts themselves, read through Europe PMC rather than through summaries of them: the
 * Cochrane review of interventions for basal cell carcinoma (52 randomised trials, 6,690 participants), the UK
 * SINS trial of imiquimod against surgery at three and five years, the Dutch three-arm trial of photodynamic
 * therapy against imiquimod against fluorouracil at one, three and five years, the Dutch randomised trial of Mohs
 * against surgical excision for facial BCC at ten years, Marcil and Stern's meta-analysis of second skin cancers,
 * Brantsch's prospective cohort of 615 squamous cell carcinomas, Schmults's ten-year cohort of 985 patients,
 * Lansbury's pooled analysis of squamous cell carcinoma treatments, Krynitz's Swedish transplant cohort and
 * Garrett's American one, and two quality-of-life studies of people who have had these cancers.
 *
 * Registered in ./index.ts and ../../../scripts/spike-sources.ts. The question sets are wired in ../questions.ts,
 * the red cards in ../red-flags.ts (cancerIds: skin-cancer, basal-cell-carcinoma, cutaneous-scc), the first 60
 * days checklists in ../first-60-days-checklists.ts, and the decision aid in ../decision-tools/bcc-low-risk-treatment.ts.
 */
