// ---------------------------------------------------------------- questions: the family page
export const skinCancerQuestions: Question[] = [
  { setting: "Newly diagnosed", question: "Which type of skin cancer is this, and is it a basal cell carcinoma, a squamous cell carcinoma or something else?", why: "Almost everything that follows turns on that answer. The British Association of Dermatologists says basal cell carcinomas very rarely spread to other parts of the body and are almost never a danger to life, and that most squamous cell carcinomas are low-risk skin cancers and can be cured, while a small number recur locally or spread to the lymph nodes. If the word used is melanoma, this page is the wrong one and the melanoma pages carry the detail." },
  { setting: "Newly diagnosed", question: "Has this been called low risk or high risk, and which features made it one or the other?", why: "Cancer Research UK lists what doctors weigh for a basal cell carcinoma: where it is on the body, how big it is, how clear and even the edges are, how many times it has been treated before, the growth pattern, and how deep it is in the skin, along with how well your immune system works and whether you have had radiotherapy to the area. The answer decides who treats you and with what." },
  { setting: "Newly diagnosed", question: "Who is going to treat this: my GP, the local hospital skin cancer team, or the specialist skin cancer team?", why: "Cancer Research UK describes two levels of multidisciplinary team for skin cancer, the local hospital skin cancer team in district general hospitals and the specialist skin cancer team in larger hospitals with cancer or plastic surgery centres, and says some GPs have had special training and can treat certain low-risk skin cancers themselves. NICE CSG8 is the service guidance that set those levels up." },
  { setting: "Newly diagnosed", question: "How long is it reasonable to wait, and what would make waiting a bad idea?", why: "Cancer Research UK says a basal cell carcinoma referral may be less urgent than for other skin cancers, that the wait can be up to a few months, and that basal cell carcinoma generally takes years to develop so waiting is unlikely to do any harm, while also saying to talk to the GP if you are worried about the wait. In Scotland the referral guidance makes an exception for a possible basal cell carcinoma close to an important structure such as the eye or the ear canal." },
  { setting: S_FACE, question: "Show me on my face where the cut will be, and tell me whether the wound will be stitched, grafted, flapped or left to heal on its own.", why: "The Mohs leaflet from the British Association of Dermatologists lists five ways of dealing with the wound: healing on its own over several weeks up to two months, stitches, a skin graft, a skin flap, or referral to another surgical team to reconstruct it. Which one you get depends on the site, the size of the wound and your own preference as well as the surgeon's, so it is a conversation and not an announcement." },
  { setting: S_FACE, question: "If a graft is needed, where will the skin be taken from, and what will that place look like afterwards?", why: "Cancer Research UK says the donor site usually comes from somewhere not too obvious such as the inner thigh, that a shaved donor site heals on its own without stitches while a cut one is stitched, and that a flap keeps its own blood supply and is taken from skin next to the wound. Two wounds rather than one is the part people are not expecting." },
  { setting: S_FACE, question: "What will this look like at six weeks, at six months and at a year?", why: "Cancer Research UK says scars are quite noticeable and red to start with but get paler and less noticeable over time, and that some can be thick and raised. The NHS says most scars fade over time, that this can take up to two years or more, and that massaging with a water-based cream, keeping the scar covered in the sun for at least a year and using SPF 30 or more all help." },
  { setting: S_FACE, question: "Will I have numbness, and will it come back?", why: "Cancer Research UK says numbness, tingling and pain in the area are due to nerve injury and may get better with time, and warns that a local anaesthetic to the middle or lower face means you cannot feel anything until it wears off, so hot food and drink should be avoided because of the risk of burns. The BAD says nerve damage is a less common risk of Mohs surgery and can rarely cause muscle weakness." },
  { setting: S_FACE, question: "Near my eye, my nose or my lip, who does the reconstruction, and will I need a second team?", why: "The BAD says Mohs surgery is normally done by a dermatologist with specialist training and that if needed other doctors can repair the wound, naming maxillofacial, oculoplastic and plastic surgeons. Cancer Research UK says only specially trained dermatologists or plastic surgeons do skin flaps and that you might need more than one operation." },
  { setting: S_MOHS, question: "How long will the day be, how many stages are likely, and can someone stay with me?", why: "The BAD leaflet says it can take up to three hours to get a result from one stage, that another stage may follow, that you could be in hospital for up to a day, and in plain words that it can be a long and tiring day. It also says you will be awake with local anaesthetic, that more anaesthetic is given for each further stage, and that in 90 percent of cases the cancer is removed after one or two stages." },
  { setting: S_MOHS, question: "Will the wound be closed on the same day, or am I going home with it open?", why: "The BAD leaflet says that once the surgeon is satisfied the cancer has gone, the options are healing on its own, stitches, a graft, a flap or referral to another team, and that once the wound is closed you will likely be able to go home. Knowing in advance which of those five is likely changes what you pack and who you arrange to collect you." },
  { setting: S_MOHS, question: "How do I get home, and what do I need to bring?", why: "The BAD says you will need someone to drive you to and from surgery and that driving and public transport are not advised afterwards. Its list of things to bring is food and drink, comfortable clothes, something to do in the waiting time, and a list of your medicines and allergies." },
  { setting: S_MOHS, question: "Is Mohs available here, and what is the wait compared with ordinary excision?", why: "The BAD names the drawbacks itself: there are usually longer waiting lists for Mohs than for standard excision because fewer cases can be done in a day, which means the cancer could grow while waiting; Mohs takes longer on the day, half a day on average; and not all skin cancers should be removed this way. Asking for it when it is not indicated is not a favour to yourself." },
  { setting: S_SUN, question: "What sunscreen should I be using now, at what strength, and how often?", why: "Cancer Research UK says to use at least SPF 30 with four or five UVA stars, generously and reapplied regularly, and that a specialist might suggest SPF 50 on exposed skin. The BAD says to apply plenty 15 to 30 minutes before going out and to reapply every two hours and straight after swimming and towel-drying, and that sunscreen is additional to clothing and shade rather than a substitute for them." },
  { setting: S_SUN, question: "I work outdoors. What does sun protection actually look like for me?", why: "NICE NG34 (1.1.1) names outdoor workers and people with outdoor hobbies as a group at increased risk that public health activity should focus on, alongside people who are immunosuppressed and people with a personal or family history of skin cancer. Cancer Research UK's practical list is close-weave cotton clothing, long sleeves and trousers, a wide-brimmed hat that shades the face and neck, sunglasses with 100 percent UV protection, and shade between 11am and 3pm." },
  { setting: S_SUN, question: "If I am covering up all the time, what happens to my vitamin D?", why: "Cancer Research UK says avoiding direct sunlight reduces vitamin D, and that the Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition recommends a daily 10 microgram supplement in the UK between October and March, and year round for people who spend long periods indoors or cover up outdoors. It says to ask the skin specialist or GP whether you should be taking one." },
  { setting: S_MIND, question: "Can I be referred for skin camouflage, and can I refer myself?", why: "The NHS says special creams and powders can be applied to make a scar less noticeable, that a GP can refer you or you can refer yourself online, that a trained professional colour-matches them to your skin and shows you how to apply them, and that you can get the products on prescription or buy them. Changing Faces is the UK charity the NHS points to." },
  { setting: S_MIND, question: "Who do I talk to if the scar, or the fear of the next one, is getting to me?", why: "The NHS says a GP can refer you for talking therapy if a scar is affecting your mental health. Cancer Research UK says specialist nurses can help if you are finding it difficult to cope, and that you may want to see a counsellor. In a study of 160 people after surgery for facial non-melanoma skin cancer, those with recurrent disease had a higher adjusted global symptom severity score and poorer psychological quality of life than those treated once." },
  { setting: S_MIND, question: "How do I check my own skin, and what am I looking for?", why: "The BAD says to check your skin for changes once a month and to ask a friend or family member to help with areas you cannot see, such as your back, and to see the GP for any mole, mark or scab that is growing, changing, bleeding or not healing, or for any change where a skin cancer was treated before. Knowing what to look for is what stops every freckle becoming a crisis." },
  { setting: S_IMMUNE, question: "I take immunosuppressants. How often should my skin be checked, and by whom?", why: "The BAD and BSSCII leaflet for transplant recipients says to check with your doctor or nurse about the frequency and setting of in-clinic skin checks, because it varies with your individual risk factors and your hospital's protocol. NICE NG34 (1.1.1) names people who are immunosuppressed among the groups who should take extra care to avoid skin damage and skin cancer." },
  { setting: S_IMMUNE, question: "Could my immunosuppression be reduced or changed because of this?", why: "The BAD and BSSCII leaflet says that in some patients with multiple cancers or more serious types of skin cancer, the dermatologist may discuss with the transplant physicians whether reducing or changing the immunosuppressant medication might reduce the number of skin cancers you go on to develop. It names azathioprine and ciclosporin as higher-risk drugs because they interact directly with ultraviolet light." },
  { setting: S_IMMUNE, question: "Should I be taking acitretin or nicotinamide to stop the next one?", why: "The BAD and BSSCII leaflet says that where patients have had multiple skin cancers, certain medicines may be added for prevention, naming the vitamin A derivative acitretin and vitamin B3 as nicotinamide. The squamous cell carcinoma leaflet adds that there is some evidence nicotinamide taken by mouth may reduce the formation of actinic keratoses, and says the specialist will have more information." },
  { setting: "Any stage", question: "Who do I ring, and when is it a 999 call rather than a phone call?", why: "The NHS lists the signs of sepsis as breathing very fast, confusion or slurred speech, blue, pale or blotchy skin, lips or tongue, a very high or very low temperature, and a rash that does not fade when pressed, and says to call 999. Cancer Research UK lists the signs of a wound infection and says to tell the team. The red cards on this page are built from those two lists." },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- questions: basal cell carcinoma
export const bccQuestions: Question[] = [
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "Which type of basal cell carcinoma is this: superficial, nodular, or one of the higher-risk kinds?", why: "The BAD says low-risk basal cell carcinomas are generally small with clearly visible edges and are the nodular or superficial subtypes, and that high-risk ones are large, have unclear edges, have grown back despite previous treatment, or are the infiltrative or morphoeic subtype. Creams, freezing and photodynamic therapy are offered for superficial disease; they are not alternatives for the others." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "If I choose a cream rather than surgery, what does that cost me in cure rate?", why: "In the UK SINS trial, 501 people with nodular or superficial basal cell carcinoma at low-risk sites were randomised to imiquimod cream or excision with a 4 mm margin. At five years, treatment succeeded in 170 of 206 in the imiquimod group (82.5 percent) and 173 of 177 in the surgery group (97.7 percent). Most imiquimod failures happened in the first year." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "Between the three non-surgical options, which one holds up best?", why: "In a Dutch trial of 601 people with superficial basal cell carcinoma, the probability of being tumour-free five years after treatment was 80.5 percent with imiquimod cream, 70.0 percent with fluorouracil cream and 62.7 percent with methyl-aminolevulinate photodynamic therapy. Imiquimod was superior to both at five years. Fluorouracil was found non-inferior to photodynamic therapy at one and three years; at five years the difference between them was not significant (hazard ratio 0.74, 95 percent confidence interval 0.53 to 1.05)." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "Which of these will look better on my face in a year?", why: "The Cochrane review found that observers rated the cosmetic result good or excellent in 60.6 percent after imiquimod against 35.6 percent after surgical excision at three years, while patients themselves rated the two the same. For photodynamic therapy against surgery at one year, patients rated 97.3 percent against 82.5 percent and observers 87.1 percent against 46.6 percent. The cosmetic advantage of the non-surgical options is real and so is their higher recurrence rate." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "What does curettage and cautery involve, and why is it being offered here?", why: "The BAD says the cancer is scraped off with a curette and the skin surface is then sealed with heat, often repeated a few times back to back, and that although it is usually not possible to test the edges of the sample as with an excision, it is generally considered effective for a low-risk basal cell carcinoma. Cancer Research UK adds there are no stitches, the area scabs over, and it may be repeated two or three times." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "What will the cream or the light actually do to my skin while it is working, and for how long?", why: "In SINS, imiquimod was used once daily for six weeks for superficial disease and twelve weeks for nodular disease, and the commonest adverse events were itching, in 211 of the imiquimod group against 129 of the surgery group, and weeping, in 160 against 81. In the Dutch three-arm trial, moderate to severe pain and a burning sensation were what patients reported most often during the photodynamic therapy session itself. Weeks of a visibly inflamed face is the price of avoiding a scar." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "Is it reasonable not to treat this at all?", why: "The BAD says in some cases it may be reasonable not to treat a basal cell carcinoma at all, for example if it is growing slowly on a non-critical area of the body or if the person would be unable to tolerate or recover from treatment because of other health problems. It is a recognised option rather than a failure to decide, and it is worth asking about rather than waiting to be offered." },
  { setting: S_BCC_CHOICE, question: "Would Mohs surgery change anything here?", why: "In a Dutch randomised trial of high-risk facial basal cell carcinoma, ten-year recurrence was 4.4 percent after Mohs and 12.2 percent after surgical excision for primary tumours, which did not reach statistical significance, and 3.9 percent against 13.5 percent for recurrent tumours, which did. The BAD says Mohs is a specialist process, is not always available, and is undertaken when standard surgery may not be suitable." },
  { setting: S_BCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "Am I being discharged, and if so, why?", why: "The BAD says not everyone needs follow-up after a basal cell carcinoma, that people are usually discharged if the risk of it coming back is low and they are not at high risk of future skin cancers, and that follow-up is considered for advanced disease, a high risk of recurrence, or a high risk of multiple skin cancers, for example people with weakened immune systems or genetic skin cancer syndromes. Being discharged is the expected outcome, not a refusal of care." },
  { setting: S_BCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "What is the chance I get another one, and when?", why: "In a meta-analysis of 17 studies, the three-year cumulative risk of a further basal cell carcinoma after a first one was 44 percent, at least a ten-fold increase over the rate of first tumours in a comparable general population, while the three-year risk of a squamous cell carcinoma after a basal cell carcinoma was 6 percent. That is the number behind the advice to check your own skin, and it is about new cancers rather than this one returning." },
  { setting: S_BCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "If it does come back, how long might that take, and does five years clear mean cured?", why: "In the ten-year Dutch trial of facial basal cell carcinoma, 56 percent of the recurrences of primary tumours happened more than five years after treatment, and the authors said this stresses the need for long-term follow-up for high-risk facial primary disease. A five-year all-clear is reassuring, but for a high-risk face lesion it is not the end of the story." },
  { setting: S_BCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "What exactly should make me ring rather than wait for the next appointment?", why: "The BAD says to see the GP if you notice any mole, mark or scab that is growing, changing, bleeding or not healing, and specifically if you are concerned about changes where a basal cell carcinoma was previously treated. Cancer Research UK says to contact the doctor or nurse about any new symptom without waiting for the next visit." },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- questions: cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma
export const csccQuestions: Question[] = [
  { setting: S_SCC_RISK, question: "How thick was it, and how wide?", why: "In a prospective study of 615 patients followed for a median of 43 months, no tumour 2.0 mm or less in thickness metastasised. Metastases occurred in 12 of 318 tumours between 2.1 and 6.0 mm thick (4 percent) and in 14 of 90 thicker than 6.0 mm (16 percent). In a separate ten-year cohort of 985 patients, a diameter of 2 cm or more carried a subhazard ratio of 7.0 for nodal metastasis. These are cohort figures, not a prediction for one person." },
  { setting: S_SCC_RISK, question: "What did the pathologist say about differentiation, depth and perineural invasion?", why: "In the ten-year cohort of 985 patients and 1,832 tumours, the independent predictors of nodal metastasis and of death from the cancer were a diameter of at least 2 cm, poor differentiation, invasion beyond the fat, and an ear or temple location, and perineural invasion was also associated with death from the cancer. Those words on the report are what decide whether you need more treatment." },
  { setting: S_SCC_RISK, question: "Does my immune system change the risk here, and by how much?", why: "In the 615-patient prospective study, immunosuppression was an independent risk factor for metastasis with a hazard ratio of 4.32, alongside increasing tumour thickness, an ear location and increasing horizontal size. The British Association of Dermatologists lists people who are immunosuppressed through medicines such as methotrexate, ciclosporin and azathioprine, through transplantation, or through conditions such as leukaemia or HIV, as being at greater risk." },
  { setting: S_SCC_RISK, question: "What are the overall numbers for a cancer like mine, and what is the outlook?", why: "In the ten-year cohort of 985 patients treated at a Boston hospital and followed for a median of 50 months, local recurrence occurred in 4.6 percent, nodal metastases in 3.7 percent, and 2.1 percent died of the cancer. The authors' own summary is that cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma carries a low but significant risk of metastasis and death. Those figures describe that cohort, not you." },
  { setting: S_SCC_RISK, question: "Is radiotherapy after surgery being considered, and why or why not?", why: "The BAD says radiotherapy can be used for squamous cell carcinoma, involves several sessions, and that for advanced disease a combination of treatments may be used. A pooled analysis of observational studies found local recurrence after external radiotherapy of 6.4 percent against 5.4 percent after standard surgical excision and 3.0 percent after Mohs, though the authors warned those comparisons carry the biases of the study designs included." },
  { setting: S_SCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "How often will I be seen, for how long, and by whom?", why: "There is a real difference between the sources here and it is worth naming. The BAD leaflet says current guidelines are that patients at low risk of a second squamous cell carcinoma do not need a specialist following them up, and that higher-risk cancers should be followed up regularly for one to two years. Cancer Research UK says a high-risk squamous cell carcinoma might mean appointments every four to six months for at least five years. Ask which your team is doing and why." },
  { setting: S_SCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "Will my lymph nodes be examined, and should they be scanned?", why: "Cancer Research UK says surgery to remove nearby lymph nodes is not a common operation for non-melanoma skin cancers but may be done if a squamous cell carcinoma has spread to them, and that for a scalp or face cancer this means the nodes on the same side of the neck. It also says follow-up may include a skin biopsy, an ultrasound scan or a CT scan to check for signs of the cancer coming back." },
  { setting: S_SCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "What is the chance I get another squamous cell carcinoma?", why: "In a meta-analysis of 17 studies, the three-year cumulative risk of a further squamous cell carcinoma after a first one was 18 percent, at least a ten-fold increase over the general population rate, and the risk of developing a basal cell carcinoma after a squamous cell carcinoma was about the same as after a basal cell carcinoma. The BAD's patient leaflet puts it higher over five years: about 40 percent after a low-risk squamous cell carcinoma and up to 80 percent after a higher-risk one." },
  { setting: S_SCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "Should the sun-damaged skin around the scar be treated too?", why: "The BAD says treating areas of scaly sun damage, meaning actinic keratosis and Bowen disease, may reduce the risk of a squamous cell carcinoma, and its transplant leaflet describes large areas of sun-damaged skin as field change which carries a greater risk of skin cancers, so early identification and treatment are recommended. This is why a dermatologist may treat skin that does not look like cancer to you." },
  { setting: S_SCC_FOLLOWUP, question: "Which new symptoms mean I should ring rather than wait?", why: "The BAD says to look out for scaly, red and raised areas that are new, increasing in size, changing in appearance, not healing as expected or forming a recurrent scab. Its transplant leaflet adds that in a person on immunosuppressants, pain in a growing skin lump is particularly suspicious for a squamous cell carcinoma. A new lump in the neck, armpit or groin on the side of a previous squamous cell carcinoma is on the red cards for this reason." },
];

